{"maxId":34,"entries":{"chapter-9-study-mental-status-and-neurologic-examination|Chapter 9 Study: Mental Status and Neurologic Examination":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Combine a detailed history with a comprehensive examination; obtain collateral history from a reliable informant when cognition or affect may compromise the account (p133–135).":["u3"],"copy|Slower processing and retrieval can accompany aging, while new learning remains possible. Delayed word recall helps distinguish cognitive impairment from normal aging (p134).":["u4"],"copy|Distal vibration loss, reduced Achilles reflexes and restricted upward gaze may occur with aging; mild muscle wasting without focal signs need not signify disease (p133–134).":["u5"],"copy|An extensor plantar response is not normal aging: it indicates upper motor neuron pathology (p133–134).":["u6"],"copy|Assess observation, cognition, function and neuropsychiatric status together; patient denial or lack of awareness makes collateral information valuable (p135).":["u7"],"copy|Altered alertness accompanies cognitive deficits and an underlying medical illness that is almost always treatable; investigate it rather than attributing it to age (p133).":["u8"],"copy|Test language through spontaneous speech, comprehension, repetition, naming, reading and writing (p133).":["u9"],"copy|Animal fluency: approximately 18 animals in 1 minute is normal; fewer than 14 is abnormal. The test is sensitive but lacks specificity (p141).":["u10"],"copy|Examine cranial nerves, strength, coordination, sensation, tendon and pathologic reflexes, and neurovascular status; inspect the head and neck as well (p142).":["u11"],"copy|Test smell with familiar odors such as coffee or vanilla, each nostril separately; avoid complex perfumes or noxious ammonia (p142).":["u12"],"copy|Review gait and station alongside strength, joint disease, sensation and coordination. Medication adverse effects and interactions are significant contributors to falls (p134).":["u13"],"copy|History and examination should generate the diagnostic hypotheses that guide laboratory, imaging and specialized studies (p133).":["u14"],"copy|Do not confuse slow processing with inability to learn, or a diminished ankle reflex with a pathologic plantar response (p134).":["u15"],"copy|Subtle age-associated memory changes do not impair normal social functioning or daily activities; more substantial cognitive decline requires evaluation (p148).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"table-9-4-book-image|Table 9-4 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Table 9-4, p.138":["u19","u34"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u20"],"copy|2026-Jun Q41":["u21","u32"],"copy|2025-Jun Q16":["u22"],"copy|2025-Jun Q34":["u23"],"copy|CHAPTER 9s / HAZZARD":["u24"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 9 - Mental Status and Neurologic Examination; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u25"],"copy|Normal aging versus disease":["u26"],"copy|Mental status assessment":["u27"],"copy|Neurologic examination":["u28"],"copy|Clinical use & exam traps":["u29"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u30"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u31"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (3):":["u33"]}}
